<description>
Erase the cross marker and add a reflected adjacent copy of the other foreground sprite.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color, and exactly two foreground colors are present.
- One foreground color forms the sprite to preserve and duplicate.
- The other foreground color forms a one-pixel-thick row/column marker with one junction and one uniquely longest arm.

Rule
- Interpret the marker's uniquely longest arm as a stem direction.
- Rotate that stem direction 90 degrees in row/column coordinates to choose the side of the sprite's bounding box.
- Mirror the sprite vertically for an above/below copy, or horizontally for a left/right copy, and paste it adjacent to that side.
- Output only the original sprite and its reflected copy; the marker is erased to background.

Key insight
- The marker is not a target location: its asymmetric arm encodes which adjacent side receives the mirrored sprite.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "foreground sprite s and asymmetric one-junction row/column marker",
  "transform": "erase the marker; mirror s across its bbox horizontally or vertically and paste the reflected copy adjacent to the side decoded from the marker",
  "symbol_instructions": "marker's uniquely longest arm, rotated 90 degrees in row/column coordinates, selects the adjacent side"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 6-12 rows x 10-13 cols, background=0 [observed]
  colors: exactly 2 foreground colors; one is the sprite (color 4 [observed]), one is the marker (color 6 [observed])
  sprite:
    bbox: 3-5 rows x 3-7 cols
    density: 50-61% of bbox filled
    symmetry: neither LR nor UD symmetric (required for non-trivial output)
    connectivity: 1-3 connected components allowed
  marker:
    shape: T or + with exactly 1 junction cell (degree >= 3)
    arms: all pixels lie on same row or column as junction (axis-aligned cross)
    longest_arm: exactly 1 arm of length 2 (uniquely longest)
    other_arms: each length 0 or 1; the two perpendicular arms are always 1; opposite arm is 0 or 1
    pixel_count: 5 (T-shape) or 6 (+-shape)
  layout:
    sprite and marker bboxes do not overlap
    min pixel distance between them >= 2
    grid has enough space adjacent to sprite bbox in the copy direction for the reflected copy (>= sprite height or width)
  transform:
    stem = direction of longest marker arm
    copy_direction = rotate stem 90 degrees via (dr,dc)->(-dc,dr)
    mirror sprite with flipud (if copy is up/down) or fliplr (if copy is left/right)
    paste reflected copy adjacent to sprite on that side; erase marker

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H x W (6-12 x 10-13).
    2. Design an asymmetric sprite (3-5 x 3-7 bbox, 50-61% density, no LR or UD symmetry).
    3. Choose a copy direction (up/down/left/right) determining the mirror axis.
    4. Build output = sprite + its reflected copy placed adjacent on that side. Place so both fit in grid with margin for marker.
    5. Derive input by keeping only the original sprite half and removing the reflected copy.
    6. Build marker with junction cell, one arm of length 2 in the stem direction (copy_direction rotated back via (dr,dc)->(dc,-dr)), perpendicular arms of length 1, opposite arm 0 or 1.
    7. Place marker on background with no bbox overlap with sprite and >= 2 pixels apart.
```
